	'pg_terms_h1' => 'Terms',
	'pg_terms_lede' => 'These pre-order terms govern build slot reservations, deposit payments, delivery timelines, and hardware warranty conditions for Zaseki Panel. All transactions are billed in euros excluding VAT.',
	'pg_thanks_h1' => 'Thanks',
	'pg_thanks_lede' => 'Thank you for sending your pre-order enquiry to Zaseki Panel. We have received your message and will review your building details promptly.',
	'pilots_b1_p1' => 'At Quayside Works, three floors accommodate 240 desks. Before the wall panel was installed, mornings created a persistent bottleneck around the reception desk. Rosa Almeida would answer the same question dozens of times before ten o\'clock: visitors and members arriving through the front doors wanted to know which floor had open seats. People would take the lift upstairs, wander down aisles between occupied workstations, spot jackets draped over chairs, and return to the ground floor frustrated. With a Zaseki Panel mounted beside reception, arriving workers glance at the wall display, tap a free desk showing soft sage, and watch it turn clay. They take the stairs knowing their seat is reserved for fifteen minutes. Rosa no longer spends her mornings managing floor navigation, and the lobby remains quiet as members move directly to their desks.',
	'pilots_b2_p1' => 'Lindenhof Commons operates a single coworking floor with 80 desks. Community manager Jonas Feld noticed that members arriving by bicycle often faced uncertainty when walking in from the rear courtyard. They would stow helmets and lock bikes in the basement store, walk up to the workspace, and discover that preferred desk clusters were already full. After mounting a Zaseki Panel in the ground-floor entry corridor, the routine changed completely. Members entering from the bike store pause at the panel, see the floor layout illuminated in sage, and tap a desk in their preferred zone. The indicator shifts to clay, securing the place while they stow their gear, fill a water bottle, and take off coats. By the time they reach the desk and tap the round tag, the indicator turns stone. If someone gets distracted talking in the hallway, the fifteen-minute hold quietly lets go, keeping the floor honest without anyone having to intervene.',
	'pilots_b3_p1' => 'Oriel House is a shared studio building where three creative practices share a single floor of 46 desks. Studio manager Amaka Obi managed an arrangement where desk sharing worked smoothly most days, except on Tuesdays when project deadlines overlapped. Team members from all three studios would arrive early to claim long tables, leaving latecomers unsure where they were permitted to sit. Placing a Zaseki Panel at the entrance established an objective, shared view of the floor. Desks are not permanently owned; instead, anyone arriving on Tuesday sees exactly which tables have open positions marked in sage. Tapping the board holds a seat, and tapping the desk tag confirms occupancy in stone. Because the status is visible to all three studios simultaneously, misunderstandings about table allocation disappeared. When people head out for client visits or meetings, unconfirmed holds release after fifteen minutes, ensuring every available workspace remains accessible to whoever needs it.',
